// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row generic_enum_type_printed_without_args (tier 4).
// Right verdict, useless sentence. A `let` type mismatch between two
// instantiations of the same generic ENUM prints both sides with their type
// ARGUMENTS DROPPED, so the message says the two types are the same:
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected Opt, got Opt
//
// ⚠ MEASURED 2026-09-08 on build 2c5a33ef99e94fc9 43, with the control that
// separates the printer from the checker: the SAME `let` mismatch over a
// generic STRUCT prints the arguments correctly —
//   struct W<T> { v: T }   let a: W<i64> = ...; let b: W<i32> = a;
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected W<i32>, got W<i64>
// so it is the generic-ENUM spelling of the type printer, not the `let` site
// and not generics as such. The defect is reachable with no coercion anywhere
// in the program (this one), which is what separates it from the row
// enum_variant_ctor_arg_no_unsize_coercion, where the same nonsense sentence
// also appears (`Opt::S(&a)` at `Opt<&[i64]>`).
//
// Provenance: found 2026-09-08 varying the shape of the enum-ctor coercion row.
package generic_enum_type_printed_without_args;
enum Opt<T> { N, S(T) }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: Opt<i64> = Opt::S(1i64);
    let b: Opt<i32> = a;
    return 0i32;
}
